# Shadowless line endings — LF everywhere, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This game BUILDS its deliverable: `tools/build.js`
# concatenates `src/*.js` and `src/style.css` into `shadowless.html`, copying
# whatever line endings each source file happens to have. And `tools/test.js`
# gates on `build --check`, which compares a fresh build against the committed
# artifact byte for byte.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine, so git STORES LF and WRITES CRLF.
# Nothing here is wrong until git writes a source file — a `git checkout`, a
# merge, a fresh clone — after which that file is CRLF on disk, the build copies
# the CRLF into the artifact, and the artifact stops matching what is committed.
#
# GIT REPORTS THE TREE CLEAN THROUGHOUT, because it normalises line endings when
# it hashes a working file and the build does not. The gate is more sensitive
# than `git status` here, which is a genuinely confusing place to be. Found in
# Job 15d, 2 Sep 2026, by doing exactly one `git checkout -- src/engine.js`.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE THE FIX: `shadowless.html` held 6,255 CRLF line ends and
# 17,572 LF ones — a patchwork, because `src/ui.js`, `src/packs.js` and
# `src/progress.js` had been checked out by git at some point and the other eight
# had not. It ran perfectly. The deliverable's exact bytes were an accident of
# which files git had most recently touched on one machine.
#
# THE COST OF NOT HAVING THIS IS PAID BY A FRESH CLONE, which is the case that
# matters: `CLAUDE.md` says local history is the only real copy, and the
# collection is meant to go up as a deliberate release. On a fresh clone every
# source file arrives CRLF, `build --check` fails on the first run, and
# rebuilding rewrites all 1.4 MB of the artifact.
#
# Scoped to this folder deliberately. Attributes apply to the directory they sit
# in and below, so no other game is affected. Asterism has its own for a related
# reason — a pre-commit hash lock that needs byte-stable checkouts — and the two
# do not interact.

*.js    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Never translated, never diffed as text. Trevor's deck workbooks are the inbox
# `tools/wants.js` reads, and a mangled one is not recoverable from a diff.
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
*.gsheet binary

# BACKUPS ARE SNAPSHOTS AND KEEP THEIR BYTES. `-text` means git neither converts
# them on checkout nor diffs them as text, so a copy taken before a job stays
# exactly the file that was copied.
#
# This is a judgement rather than a technicality, and it went the other way
# first. Normalising them is *consistent* — but it rewrites six 1.4 MB HTML
# snapshots for 35,000 lines of diff that change nothing you could see, and a
# backup whose bytes have been edited to match a later convention is a slightly
# worse backup. The live tree is the thing that has to be uniform, because the
# live tree is what gets built.
#
# It also settles a case where the bytes ARE the content. Until 12 Aug 2026
# `tools/build.js` searched for a LITERAL NUL, making it the very thing its own
# guard warns about — grep called it binary and refused to search it.
# `backups/pre-job6` and `pre-job7` hold the last copies of that file in that
# state. Running a line-ending filter over a file whose defect is a stray byte is
# how the evidence of a bug gets tidied away.
backups/** -text
